   "text": {
    "L1": "The Gupta dynasty rose in the Ganga plains around 320 CE under Chandragupta I.",
    "L2": "Creativity grew through royal patronage, wealth from farming and trade, relative peace, and centres of learning.",
    "L3": "Aryabhata wrote the Aryabhatiya in 499 CE and explained the Earth's spin, eclipses by shadows, place value, and pi.",
    "L4": "Metal-workers made the iron pillar at Mehrauli, which has hardly rusted in about 1,600 years.",
    "L5": "Kalidasa wrote Sanskrit plays and poems, while Ajanta paintings and early stone temples showed artistic skill.",
    "L6": "An eclipse is not the Sun or Moon being switched off; shadows explain what we see.",
    "L7": "Calling this a golden age needs care: court poets and farmers might have described very different lives.",
    "L8": "Now put four Gupta rulers in the order of the dynasty's rise."
   },
